|> body

    Some thought reveals that this value of $x$
    would be _minus one-quarter the circumference
    of a unit circle_, because $(0, -1)$ is 
    one-quarter of the unit circle clockwise from $(1, 0)$. Note
    that one-quarter the circumference of a unit circle
    was estimated to be
    $$
    \approx {11\over 7} = 
    %6.\overline{285714}
    1.\overline{571428}
    $$
    in Exercise 24 of Chapter 3 (by direct inspection of
    the graph $y = \cos(x)$, for that matter, which may
    or may not be cheating), which
    agrees with the visual estimate $x \approx -1.6$.
    (But that value would be namely 
    $x = -1.57...$,
    not $x = -1.6$.)
